Six weeks ago I wrote [a note about going down the stack](/posts/20260626-down-the-stack/): SheepShaver booted Mac OS 9.0.4 on Apple Silicon with a native <abbr title="Just-In-Time compilation">JIT</abbr>, and 9.2 was the thing I still could not do. That post ended with "I think I could crack 9.2, too."

It boots.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - A fork of SheepShaver, not QEMU, reaches a settled Mac OS 9.2.1 Finder desktop through the genuine New World <abbr title="Read-Only Memory">ROM</abbr> boot path with the ARM64 JIT on, against one verified baseline and three accepted ROM identities
> - The project has a name, NewSheep, and a launcher, SiliconSheep, that can install 9.2.2 from its own installer media and boot the result
> - Boot is 28.3 seconds on the verified baseline, and sub-10 with a newer technique I am still validating; no measured comparison against UTM exists yet
> - Photoshop 7.0.1, Ultra Pinball and AppleWorks are driven under automation, not just launched
> - It only gets exercised on my machine and my use case, so if this is your kind of thing I would like to hear from you

[New World](https://web.archive.org/web/20041011114718/http://developer.apple.com/technotes/tn/tn1167.html) is the boot firmware Apple shipped from [1998/9](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Macintosh_G3#Blue_and_White) onward. The earlier ["Old World" ROM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_World_ROM) was mostly a library the operating system called into; New World expects to find a real machine underneath it, and goes looking. And as I lamented in [my last post](/posts/20260626-down-the-stack/), getting New World working has been a much bigger challenge.

In the end I've made a hybrid between QEMU and SheepShaver, hopefully landing the best of both worlds and not the worst of both. I think it is a good foundation to build on.

Running Mac OS 9.2 on an Apple Silicon Mac is not itself news. [UTM](https://mac.getutm.app/) has done it for a while, and if you just want 9.2 today, that is still the sensible route. But QEMU underneath UTM simulates a whole Macintosh, every chip the original had, which is why it feels like a 300 MHz G3. NewSheep preserves SheepShaver's method: patch the ROM, run guest threads as host threads, treat guest memory as pointer arithmetic into the host's address space. It is much faster and much less general. NewSheep's new thing is to provide a "just enough" hardware layer.

## What's done so far

The fork has a name now, NewSheep, because at some point "the macemu JIT branch" stopped describing it. Same chain as before ([cebix](https://github.com/cebix/macemu) → [kanjitalk755](https://github.com/kanjitalk755/macemu) → [rcarmo](https://github.com/rcarmo/macemu-jit) → this).

**A real install lifecycle.** You can now take your New World ROM and a 9.2.2 installer ISO you own, create a virtual machine, initialize a blank disk in Drive Setup, run Install Mac OS, shut down properly from the Special menu, and boot the thing you just installed. Yay! (9.2.2 is what the installer produces; 9.2.1 is the version with the verified boot baseline behind it.)

**A launcher that behaves like a product.** SiliconSheep is a desktop app, built with Tauri, that owns virtual machines as first-class objects: create, import an existing 9.2.2 system disk, preflight, launch, and an embedded Console for the guest display rather than SheepShaver's raw window.

**Testing that runs the whole operating system.** An end-to-end harness drives the guest over <abbr title="Virtual Network Computing">VNC</abbr>, waits for the Finder to actually settle rather than merely appear, injects input, and makes Mac OS run its own shutdown. Without it I would not trust any of the claims in this post.

**Real applications, driven.** Three of them are exercised rather than merely launched: Photoshop 7.0.1 opens a generated TIFF and applies an Unsharp Mask; Ultra Pinball takes gameplay input in a verified game window; AppleWorks gets a turn too.

**A 3D detour.** (3-D-tour?) I spent two weeks on ATI Rage 128 acceleration, and it got further than I expected. Deus Ex launches, gets through its splash screen and renders actual geometry.

